output:
{
  "title": "Before this becomes load-bearing: four constraints on the self-hosted service",
  "purpose": "To get a decision on record for each of the four measured constraints before the service is depended on by anyone but its author. Answers become the deployment checklist and get written back into docs/SELF-HOSTING.md as the agreed operating envelope — including who owns link revocation and what MAX_AWAIT_MS is set to behind our proxy.",
  "resumeHint": "Once answers are in: set MAX_AWAIT_MS and LINK_TTL_HOURS in the Deployment env, confirm replicas: 1 with strategy Recreate, verify cf-cache-status on /mcp, then deploy. Record the outcomes in docs/SELF-HOSTING.md under 'Constraints that are measured, not theoretical'.",
  "visibility": "open",
  "participants": [
    {
      "name": "Service owner",
      "role": "Owns the deployment and will be paged when it breaks"
    },
    {
      "name": "Platform / infra",
      "role": "Owns the ingress, the tunnel, and anything that caches"
    },
    {
      "name": "Security reviewer",
      "role": "Signs off on the identity model and the replay window"
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "Read this before you deploy, not after",
        "lead": "Four constraints govern this service. None of them were designed in — each was found by something failing in a way that did not look like failure. Three of the four fail silently: the service keeps returning 200s while replay protection is off, or callers get 524s with no body, or every agent installs a client that is one deploy old. This page states each constraint, then asks you to decide the thing that constraint leaves open.",
        "points": [
          "Exactly one replica. Horizontal scaling does not degrade replay protection, it removes it.",
          "MAX_AWAIT_MS must sit below whatever your proxy's response ceiling is. Ours is ~100s; the default is 90s.",
          "A CDN in front of this may cache by file extension no matter what headers you send. The canonical client URL is /mcp, with no extension.",
          "Participant links are capabilities, not identities. This is deliberate. It is not an audit trail and cannot be configured into one."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The four constraints, as measured",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "Run exactly one replica",
            "detail": "The replay-protection nonce store is in memory (src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts). Every signed request carries a nonce accepted once, which is what stops a captured request being replayed inside its five-minute freshness window. With two replicas, a nonce burned on one is unknown to the other, and a replayed request routed to the second one succeeds. The Deployment pins replicas: 1 with a Recreate strategy. Scaling out is possible, but it means moving the nonce store to Postgres or Redis first — the moment replicas changes, that has to change with it."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Long polls die at about 100 seconds behind Cloudflare",
            "detail": "Cloudflare gives an origin roughly 100 seconds to respond before answering 524 itself. Measured through the tunnel: a 150-second poll died at 125 seconds with HTTP 524 and no body. Not a slow response — no response, and no way for the caller to distinguish a dead wait from an empty one. Holding a connection past that ceiling does not make the caller wait longer, it makes the wait fail. MAX_AWAIT_MS defaults to 90 seconds so every poll returns inside the window; one that exhausts its budget returns timedOut: true with whatever has arrived. A timeout is a legitimate outcome, not an error, and the MCP client loops shorter polls so a caller asking for five minutes gets five minutes."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A CDN may cache by file extension regardless of what you send",
            "detail": "/mcp.js was served with cache-control: no-store and came back from the edge with cf-cache-status: HIT and an injected max-age=14400. Cloudflare caches .js by default, by extension, whatever the origin intended. For four hours after every deploy, agents installed the previous client. The fix is in the path, not the headers: the canonical URL is /mcp, no extension. /mcp.js remains as an alias for anyone who saved the old URL. Origin headers are a request, not an instruction — and this failure is silent, because everything works, one deploy behind."
          },
          {
            "tone": "info",
            "title": "Participant links are capabilities",
            "detail": "Anyone holding a participant link can answer as that participant. A forwarded link is a forwarded identity. This is why the product works at all: participants need no account, no email verification, and no Claude access. Attribution is whichever link was used, which costs nothing and is honest about what it is. It is not an audit trail — if responses ever need to be contested, that is the point to add verified identity, and that is a change to this service, not a switch waiting to be flipped."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "The knobs that exist, and what they actually bound",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "replicas",
            "value": "1, strategy Recreate. Not tunable without first moving the nonce store out of process."
          },
          {
            "key": "Nonce freshness window",
            "value": "5 minutes. In-memory, single-process (src/adapters/crypto/nonces.ts)."
          },
          {
            "key": "MAX_AWAIT_MS",
            "value": "Default 90000. Must be below the response ceiling of whatever proxy sits in front."
          },
          {
            "key": "Cloudflare 524 ceiling (measured)",
            "value": "~100s advertised; observed kill at 125s on a 150s poll, HTTP 524, no body."
          },
          {
            "key": "Canonical MCP client URL",
            "value": "/mcp — no extension. /mcp.js is an alias only."
          },
          {
            "key": "LINK_TTL_HOURS",
            "value": "Bounds how long a link stays answerable. Expiry is checked inside the token-resolution query, not after it."
          },
          {
            "key": "Revocation",
            "value": "revoked_at exists in the schema and is honoured on every resolve. Nothing exposes it over HTTP — killing a link today is a manual UPDATE."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two things you will actually need to type",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The constraint, as it appears in the Deployment. If this diff ever changes, the nonce store has to have moved first.",
            "language": "yaml",
            "code": "spec:\n  replicas: 1\n  strategy:\n    type: Recreate"
          },
          {
            "note": "Revoking a single link today. There is no endpoint for this; it is a direct write, and it takes effect on the next resolve.",
            "language": "sql",
            "code": "UPDATE participant_links\n   SET revoked_at = now()\n WHERE token = $1;"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "divider"
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "What we need decided before deploy"
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "Answer these before the first team dependency lands on this service, not after the first incident. Three of the four failure modes above return 200s while broken."
    },
    {
      "widget": "pick-one",
      "props": {
        "title": "The replica constraint",
        "context": "replicas: 1 is the single most important operational constraint here. It is currently enforced only by the manifest, which means an autoscaler, a hasty scale-up during an incident, or a copied Deployment removes replay protection with no visible symptom.",
        "question": "How do we stop replicas ever going above 1 by accident?",
        "options": [
          {
            "value": "manifest-only",
            "label": "Manifest only — accept the risk, document it, move on"
          },
          {
            "value": "policy-guard",
            "label": "Add an admission policy / OPA rule that rejects replicas > 1 for this Deployment"
          },
          {
            "value": "startup-check",
            "label": "Make the service itself detect peers and refuse to start (or alarm loudly)"
          },
          {
            "value": "fix-properly",
            "label": "Move the nonce store to Postgres/Redis now, before this is load-bearing"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "The timeout ceiling and the edge",
        "intro": "Whatever sits in front of this service decides the real ceiling; the service only decides whether it respects it. We need the number for our path, and confirmation that nothing in front of us caches by extension.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "proxy_chain",
            "label": "What is actually in front of this service in our deployment (tunnel, LB, WAF, CDN — in order)?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "max_await_ms",
            "label": "What should MAX_AWAIT_MS be set to (milliseconds), given that chain's response ceiling?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "ceiling_evidence",
            "label": "How was that ceiling established — documented, or measured? If measured, what did the failing request look like?",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "kind": "boolean",
            "id": "cdn_checked",
            "label": "Has someone confirmed by request what our edge does with /mcp — i.e. checked cf-cache-status (or equivalent) on a real response?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "textarea",
            "id": "cdn_notes",
            "label": "Any extension-based or path-based caching rules in front of us that we should know about?",
            "required": false
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "approval",
      "props": {
        "title": "The identity model",
        "proposal": "Accept that participant links are bearer capabilities — a forwarded link is a forwarded identity — and deploy this for our use case without verified identity.",
        "detail": "This is the trade that makes the product usable: no accounts, no email verification, no Claude access required for participants. Attribution is whichever link was used. It is honest about what it is, but it is not an audit trail, and nothing in it will survive a contested response. Approve only if no decision we route through this service will need to be defended later against 'that wasn't me'. Rejecting means verified identity is a prerequisite, and that is a change to this service rather than a configuration option."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "survey",
      "props": {
        "title": "The window we do control",
        "intro": "LINK_TTL_HOURS bounds how long a link stays answerable, and revoked_at is honoured on every resolve — but nothing exposes revocation over HTTP, so killing a link today is a manual UPDATE against participant_links.",
        "questions": [
          {
            "kind": "number",
            "id": "link_ttl_hours",
            "label": "What should LINK_TTL_HOURS be for our deployment?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "id": "revocation_owner",
            "label": "Who has database access and is on the hook for revoking a leaked link out of hours?",
            "required": true
          },
          {
            "kind": "select",
            "id": "revocation_priority",
            "label": "Do we need an HTTP revocation path before this goes load-bearing?",
            "required": true,
            "options": [
              {
                "value": "before-deploy",
                "label": "Yes — blocker, build it first"
              },
              {
                "value": "soon-after",
                "label": "Yes, but a runbook with the UPDATE is enough for now"
              },
              {
                "value": "not-needed",
                "label": "No — TTL is short enough that revocation is theoretical"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "ask",
      "props": {
        "title": "Last look",
        "context": "Each of these four constraints was found rather than anticipated. The list is therefore evidence of what we have hit so far, not proof of what is left.",
        "field": {
          "kind": "textarea",
          "id": "residual_concerns",
          "label": "What would you check before signing this off that isn't on this page?",
          "required": false
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "closeWhen": {
    "kind": "all"
  }
}
